WEBVTT

00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:03.272
The Monkey’s Paw, by W. W. Jacobs.

00:00:03.272 --> 00:00:11.631
Without, the night was cold and wet, but in the small parlour of Laburnam Villa the blinds were drawn and the fire burned brightly.

00:00:11.631 --> 00:00:27.460
Father and son were at chess, the former, who possessed ideas about the game involving radical changes, putting his king into such sharp and unnecessary perils that it even provoked comment from the white-haired old lady knitting placidly by the fire.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:29.235
“Hark at the wind,” said Mr.

00:00:29.235 --> 00:00:37.659
White, who, having seen a fatal mistake after it was too late, was amiably desirous of preventing his son from seeing it.

00:00:37.659 --> 00:00:43.033
“I’m listening,” said the latter, grimly surveying the board as he stretched out his hand.

00:00:43.033 --> 00:00:43.649
“Check.”

00:00:43.649 --> 00:00:49.534
“I should hardly think that he’d come to-night,” said his father, with his hand poised over the board.

00:00:49.534 --> 00:00:51.500
“Mate,” replied the son.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:54.470
“That’s the worst of living so far out,” bawled Mr.

00:00:54.470 --> 00:01:03.493
White, with sudden and unlooked-for violence; “of all the beastly, slushy, out-of-the-way places to live in, this is the worst.

00:01:03.493 --> 00:01:06.379
Pathway’s a bog, and the road’s a torrent.

00:01:06.379 --> 00:01:08.671
I don’t know what people are thinking about.

00:01:08.671 --> 00:01:13.895
I suppose because only two houses in the road are let, they think it doesn’t matter.”

00:01:13.895 --> 00:01:19.585
“Never mind, dear,” said his wife, soothingly; “perhaps you’ll win the next one.”

00:01:19.585 --> 00:01:20.006
Mr.

00:01:20.006 --> 00:01:26.072
White looked up sharply, just in time to intercept a knowing glance between mother and son.

00:01:26.072 --> 00:01:31.856
The words died away on his lips, and he hid a guilty grin in his thin grey beard.

00:01:31.856 --> 00:01:38.726
“There he is,” said Herbert White, as the gate banged to loudly and heavy footsteps came toward the door.

00:01:38.726 --> 00:01:45.694
The old man rose with hospitable haste, and opening the door, was heard condoling with the new arrival.

00:01:45.694 --> 00:01:49.109
The new arrival also condoled with himself, so that Mrs.

00:01:49.109 --> 00:01:59.754
White said, “Tut, tut!” and coughed gently as her husband entered the room, followed by a tall, burly man, beady of eye and rubicund of visage.

00:01:59.754 --> 00:02:03.210
“Sergeant-Major Morris,” he said, introducing him.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:14.863
The sergeant-major shook hands, and taking the proffered seat by the fire, watched contentedly while his host got out whiskey and tumblers and stood a small copper kettle on the fire.

00:02:14.863 --> 00:02:32.424
At the third glass his eyes got brighter, and he began to talk, the little family circle regarding with eager interest this visitor from distant parts, as he squared his broad shoulders in the chair and spoke of wild scenes and doughty deeds; of wars and plagues and strange peoples.

00:02:32.424 --> 00:02:34.453
“Twenty-one years of it,” said Mr.

00:02:34.453 --> 00:02:37.603
White, nodding at his wife and son.

00:02:37.603 --> 00:02:41.588
“When he went away he was a slip of a youth in the warehouse.

00:02:41.588 --> 00:02:42.642
Now look at him.”

00:02:42.642 --> 00:02:45.318
“He don’t look to have taken much harm,” said Mrs.

00:02:45.318 --> 00:02:47.217
White, politely.

00:02:47.217 --> 00:02:52.851
“I’d like to go to India myself,” said the old man, “just to look round a bit, you know.”

00:02:52.851 --> 00:02:57.277
“Better where you are,” said the sergeant-major, shaking his head.

00:02:57.277 --> 00:03:02.072
He put down the empty glass, and sighing softly, shook it again.

00:03:02.072 --> 00:03:07.343
“I should like to see those old temples and fakirs and jugglers,” said the old man.

00:03:07.343 --> 00:03:12.725
“What was that you started telling me the other day about a monkey’s paw or something, Morris?”

00:03:12.725 --> 00:03:15.483
“Nothing,” said the soldier, hastily.

00:03:15.483 --> 00:03:17.374
“Leastways nothing worth hearing.”

00:03:17.374 --> 00:03:19.341
“Monkey’s paw?” said Mrs.

00:03:19.341 --> 00:03:20.936
White, curiously.

00:03:20.936 --> 00:03:27.591
“Well, it’s just a bit of what you might call magic, perhaps,” said the sergeant-major, offhandedly.

00:03:28.206 --> 00:03:31.221
His three listeners leaned forward eagerly.

00:03:31.221 --> 00:03:37.297
The visitor absent-mindedly put his empty glass to his lips and then set it down again.

00:03:37.297 --> 00:03:39.125
His host filled it for him.

00:03:39.125 --> 00:03:46.876
“To look at,” said the sergeant-major, fumbling in his pocket, “it’s just an ordinary little paw, dried to a mummy.”

00:03:46.876 --> 00:03:49.892
He took something out of his pocket and proffered it.

00:03:49.892 --> 00:03:50.332
Mrs.

00:03:50.332 --> 00:03:55.948
White drew back with a grimace, but her son, taking it, examined it curiously.

00:03:55.948 --> 00:03:58.957
“And what is there special about it?” inquired Mr.

00:03:58.957 --> 00:04:04.362
White as he took it from his son, and having examined it, placed it upon the table.

00:04:04.362 --> 00:04:10.375
“It had a spell put on it by an old fakir,” said the sergeant-major, “a very holy man.

00:04:10.375 --> 00:04:17.371
He wanted to show that fate ruled people’s lives, and that those who interfered with it did so to their sorrow.

00:04:17.371 --> 00:04:22.585
He put a spell on it so that three separate men could each have three wishes from it.”

00:04:22.585 --> 00:04:28.903
His manner was so impressive that his hearers were conscious that their light laughter jarred somewhat.

00:04:28.903 --> 00:04:34.175
“Well, why don’t you have three, sir?” said Herbert White, cleverly.

00:04:34.175 --> 00:04:39.881
The soldier regarded him in the way that middle age is wont to regard presumptuous youth.

00:04:39.881 --> 00:04:44.327
“I have,” he said, quietly, and his blotchy face whitened.

00:04:44.327 --> 00:04:47.814
“And did you really have the three wishes granted?” asked Mrs.

00:04:47.814 --> 00:04:48.926
White.

00:04:48.926 --> 00:04:54.205
“I did,” said the sergeant-major, and his glass tapped against his strong teeth.

00:04:54.205 --> 00:04:57.768
“And has anybody else wished?” persisted the old lady.

00:04:57.768 --> 00:05:00.211
“The first man had his three wishes.

00:05:00.211 --> 00:05:06.193
Yes,” was the reply; “I don’t know what the first two were, but the third was for death.

00:05:06.193 --> 00:05:07.853
That’s how I got the paw.”

00:05:07.853 --> 00:05:12.063
His tones were so grave that a hush fell upon the group.

00:05:12.063 --> 00:05:18.694
“If you’ve had your three wishes, it’s no good to you now, then, Morris,” said the old man at last.

00:05:18.694 --> 00:05:20.175
“What do you keep it for?”

00:05:20.175 --> 00:05:22.078
The soldier shook his head.

00:05:22.078 --> 00:05:25.497
“Fancy, I suppose,” he said, slowly.

00:05:25.497 --> 00:05:29.282
“I did have some idea of selling it, but I don’t think I will.

00:05:29.282 --> 00:05:31.832
It has caused enough mischief already.

00:05:31.832 --> 00:05:34.126
Besides, people won’t buy.

00:05:34.126 --> 00:05:41.253
They think it’s a fairy tale; some of them, and those who do think anything of it want to try it first and pay me afterward.”

00:05:41.253 --> 00:05:46.918
“If you could have another three wishes,” said the old man, eyeing him keenly, “would you have them?”

00:05:46.918 --> 00:05:48.841
“I don’t know,” said the other.

00:05:48.841 --> 00:05:49.851
“I don’t know.”

00:05:49.851 --> 00:05:56.512
He took the paw, and dangling it between his forefinger and thumb, suddenly threw it upon the fire.

00:05:56.512 --> 00:06:01.209
White, with a slight cry, stooped down and snatched it off.

00:06:01.209 --> 00:06:04.613
“Better let it burn,” said the soldier, solemnly.

00:06:04.613 --> 00:06:08.658
“If you don’t want it, Morris,” said the other, “give it to me.”

00:06:08.658 --> 00:06:11.209
“I won’t,” said his friend, doggedly.

00:06:11.209 --> 00:06:12.956
“I threw it on the fire.

00:06:12.956 --> 00:06:15.809
If you keep it, don’t blame me for what happens.

00:06:15.809 --> 00:06:18.859
Pitch it on the fire again like a sensible man.”

00:06:18.859 --> 00:06:22.792
The other shook his head and examined his new possession closely.

00:06:22.792 --> 00:06:25.051
“How do you do it?” he inquired.

00:06:25.051 --> 00:06:31.815
“Hold it up in your right hand and wish aloud,” said the sergeant-major, “but I warn you of the consequences.”

00:06:31.815 --> 00:06:34.315
“Sounds like the Arabian Nights,” said Mrs.

00:06:34.315 --> 00:06:37.961
White, as she rose and began to set the supper.

00:06:37.961 --> 00:06:41.650
“Don’t you think you might wish for four pairs of hands for me?”

00:06:42.265 --> 00:06:52.375
Her husband drew the talisman from pocket, and then all three burst into laughter as the sergeant-major, with a look of alarm on his face, caught him by the arm.

00:06:52.375 --> 00:06:57.220
“If you must wish,” he said, gruffly, “wish for something sensible.”

00:06:57.220 --> 00:06:57.640
Mr.

00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:03.556
White dropped it back in his pocket, and placing chairs, motioned his friend to the table.

00:07:03.556 --> 00:07:15.001
In the business of supper the talisman was partly forgotten, and afterward the three sat listening in an enthralled fashion to a second instalment of the soldier’s adventures in India.

00:07:15.001 --> 00:07:28.065
“If the tale about the monkey’s paw is not more truthful than those he has been telling us,” said Herbert, as the door closed behind their guest, just in time for him to catch the last train, “we sha’nt make much out of it.”

00:07:28.065 --> 00:07:31.547
“Did you give him anything for it, father?” inquired Mrs.

00:07:31.547 --> 00:07:34.571
White, regarding her husband closely.

00:07:34.571 --> 00:07:37.565
“A trifle,” said he, colouring slightly.

00:07:37.565 --> 00:07:40.037
“He didn’t want it, but I made him take it.

00:07:40.037 --> 00:07:42.630
And he pressed me again to throw it away.”

00:07:42.630 --> 00:07:45.921
“Likely,” said Herbert, with pretended horror.

00:07:45.921 --> 00:07:49.771
“Why, we’re going to be rich, and famous and happy.

00:07:49.771 --> 00:07:54.919
Wish to be an emperor, father, to begin with; then you can’t be henpecked.”

00:07:54.919 --> 00:07:58.479
He darted round the table, pursued by the maligned Mrs.

00:07:58.479 --> 00:08:01.277
White armed with an antimacassar.

00:08:01.277 --> 00:08:01.707
Mr.

00:08:01.707 --> 00:08:05.911
White took the paw from his pocket and eyed it dubiously.

00:08:05.911 --> 00:08:10.356
“I don’t know what to wish for, and that’s a fact,” he said, slowly.

00:08:10.356 --> 00:08:12.628
“It seems to me I’ve got all I want.”

00:08:12.628 --> 00:08:19.475
“If you only cleared the house, you’d be quite happy, wouldn’t you?” said Herbert, with his hand on his shoulder.

00:08:19.475 --> 00:08:24.868
“Well, wish for two hundred pounds, then; that ’ll just do it.”

00:08:24.868 --> 00:08:39.075
His father, smiling shamefacedly at his own credulity, held up the talisman, as his son, with a solemn face, somewhat marred by a wink at his mother, sat down at the piano and struck a few impressive chords.

00:08:39.075 --> 00:08:43.419
“I wish for two hundred pounds,” said the old man distinctly.

00:08:43.419 --> 00:08:50.085
A fine crash from the piano greeted the words, interrupted by a shuddering cry from the old man.

00:08:50.085 --> 00:08:52.410
His wife and son ran toward him.

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:58.242
“It moved,” he cried, with a glance of disgust at the object as it lay on the floor.

00:08:58.242 --> 00:09:01.861
“As I wished, it twisted in my hand like a snake.”

00:09:01.861 --> 00:09:08.757
“Well, I don’t see the money,” said his son as he picked it up and placed it on the table, “and I bet I never shall.”

00:09:08.757 --> 00:09:14.033
“It must have been your fancy, father,” said his wife, regarding him anxiously.

00:09:14.033 --> 00:09:15.307
He shook his head.

00:09:15.307 --> 00:09:20.594
“Never mind, though; there’s no harm done, but it gave me a shock all the same.”

00:09:20.594 --> 00:09:25.152
They sat down by the fire again while the two men finished their pipes.

00:09:25.152 --> 00:09:32.781
Outside, the wind was higher than ever, and the old man started nervously at the sound of a door banging upstairs.

00:09:32.781 --> 00:09:40.840
A silence unusual and depressing settled upon all three, which lasted until the old couple rose to retire for the night.

00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:54.247
“I expect you’ll find the cash tied up in a big bag in the middle of your bed,” said Herbert, as he bade them good-night, “and something horrible squatting up on top of the wardrobe watching you as you pocket your ill-gotten gains.”

00:09:54.862 --> 00:10:00.671
He sat alone in the darkness, gazing at the dying fire, and seeing faces in it.

00:10:00.671 --> 00:10:06.207
The last face was so horrible and so simian that he gazed at it in amazement.

00:10:06.207 --> 00:10:14.406
It got so vivid that, with a little uneasy laugh, he felt on the table for a glass containing a little water to throw over it.

00:10:14.406 --> 00:10:21.291
His hand grasped the monkey’s paw, and with a little shiver he wiped his hand on his coat and went up to bed.

00:10:21.291 --> 00:10:27.978
In the brightness of the wintry sun next morning as it streamed over the breakfast table he laughed at his fears.

00:10:27.978 --> 00:10:41.507
There was an air of prosaic wholesomeness about the room which it had lacked on the previous night, and the dirty, shrivelled little paw was pitched on the sideboard with a carelessness which betokened no great belief in its virtues.

00:10:41.507 --> 00:10:44.710
“I suppose all old soldiers are the same,” said Mrs.

00:10:44.710 --> 00:10:45.864
White.

00:10:45.864 --> 00:10:48.831
“The idea of our listening to such nonsense!

00:10:48.831 --> 00:10:51.484
How could wishes be granted in these days?

00:10:51.484 --> 00:10:55.972
And if they could, how could two hundred pounds hurt you, father?”

00:10:55.972 --> 00:11:00.265
“Might drop on his head from the sky,” said the frivolous Herbert.

00:11:00.265 --> 00:11:07.864
“Morris said the things happened so naturally,” said his father, “that you might if you so wished attribute it to coincidence.”

00:11:07.864 --> 00:11:13.553
“Well, don’t break into the money before I come back,” said Herbert as he rose from the table.

00:11:13.553 --> 00:11:19.391
“I’m afraid it’ll turn you into a mean, avaricious man, and we shall have to disown you.”

00:11:19.391 --> 00:11:29.781
His mother laughed, and following him to the door, watched him down the road; and returning to the breakfast table, was very happy at the expense of her husband’s credulity.

00:11:29.781 --> 00:11:43.284
All of which did not prevent her from scurrying to the door at the postman’s knock, nor prevent her from referring somewhat shortly to retired sergeant-majors of bibulous habits when she found that the post brought a tailor’s bill.

00:11:43.284 --> 00:11:50.646
“Herbert will have some more of his funny remarks, I expect, when he comes home,” she said, as they sat at dinner.

00:11:50.646 --> 00:11:52.162
“I dare say,” said Mr.

00:11:52.162 --> 00:11:59.195
White, pouring himself out some beer; “but for all that, the thing moved in my hand; that I’ll swear to.”

00:11:59.195 --> 00:12:02.505
“You thought it did,” said the old lady soothingly.

00:12:02.505 --> 00:12:04.965
“I say it did,” replied the other.

00:12:04.965 --> 00:12:08.853
“There was no thought about it; I had just—What’s the matter?”

00:12:08.853 --> 00:12:10.881
His wife made no reply.

00:12:10.881 --> 00:12:21.101
She was watching the mysterious movements of a man outside, who, peering in an undecided fashion at the house, appeared to be trying to make up his mind to enter.

00:12:21.101 --> 00:12:29.870
In mental connection with the two hundred pounds, she noticed that the stranger was well dressed, and wore a silk hat of glossy newness.

00:12:29.870 --> 00:12:33.894
Three times he paused at the gate, and then walked on again.

00:12:33.894 --> 00:12:41.023
The fourth time he stood with his hand upon it, and then with sudden resolution flung it open and walked up the path.

00:12:41.023 --> 00:12:41.459
Mrs.

00:12:41.459 --> 00:12:52.168
White at the same moment placed her hands behind her, and hurriedly unfastening the strings of her apron, put that useful article of apparel beneath the cushion of her chair.

00:12:52.168 --> 00:12:56.353
She brought the stranger, who seemed ill at ease, into the room.

00:12:56.353 --> 00:13:08.644
He gazed at her furtively, and listened in a preoccupied fashion as the old lady apologized for the appearance of the room, and her husband’s coat, a garment which he usually reserved for the garden.

00:13:08.644 --> 00:13:16.413
She then waited as patiently as her sex would permit, for him to broach his business, but he was at first strangely silent.

00:13:16.413 --> 00:13:23.198
“I—was asked to call,” he said at last, and stooped and picked a piece of cotton from his trousers.

00:13:23.198 --> 00:13:25.593
“I come from ‘Maw and Meggins.’”

00:13:26.208 --> 00:13:27.816
The old lady started.

00:13:27.816 --> 00:13:31.188
“Is anything the matter?” she asked, breathlessly.

00:13:31.188 --> 00:13:33.143
“Has anything happened to Herbert?

00:13:33.143 --> 00:13:33.929
What is it?

00:13:33.929 --> 00:13:34.716
What is it?”

00:13:34.716 --> 00:13:36.479
Her husband interposed.

00:13:36.479 --> 00:13:39.928
“There, there, mother,” he said, hastily.

00:13:39.928 --> 00:13:42.746
“Sit down, and don’t jump to conclusions.

00:13:42.746 --> 00:13:48.070
You’ve not brought bad news, I’m sure, sir;” and he eyed the other wistfully.

00:13:48.070 --> 00:13:50.404
“I’m sorry—” began the visitor.

00:13:50.404 --> 00:13:53.679
“Is he hurt?” demanded the mother, wildly.

00:13:53.679 --> 00:13:55.754
The visitor bowed in assent.

00:13:55.754 --> 00:14:00.439
“Badly hurt,” he said, quietly, “but he is not in any pain.”

00:14:00.439 --> 00:14:05.276
“Oh, thank God!” said the old woman, clasping her hands.

00:14:05.276 --> 00:14:06.511
“Thank God for that!

00:14:06.511 --> 00:14:07.118
Thank—”

00:14:07.118 --> 00:14:16.665
She broke off suddenly as the sinister meaning of the assurance dawned upon her and she saw the awful confirmation of her fears in the other’s averted face.

00:14:16.665 --> 00:14:23.121
She caught her breath, and turning to her slower-witted husband, laid her trembling old hand upon his.

00:14:23.121 --> 00:14:25.104
There was a long silence.

00:14:25.104 --> 00:14:29.898
“He was caught in the machinery,” said the visitor at length in a low voice.

00:14:29.898 --> 00:14:32.218
“Caught in the machinery,” repeated Mr.

00:14:32.218 --> 00:14:35.331
White, in a dazed fashion, “yes.”

00:14:35.331 --> 00:14:46.111
He sat staring blankly out at the window, and taking his wife’s hand between his own, pressed it as he had been wont to do in their old courting-days nearly forty years before.

00:14:46.111 --> 00:14:51.046
“He was the only one left to us,” he said, turning gently to the visitor.

00:14:51.046 --> 00:14:52.055
“It is hard.”

00:14:52.055 --> 00:14:56.310
The other coughed, and rising, walked slowly to the window.

00:14:56.310 --> 00:15:03.159
“The firm wished me to convey their sincere sympathy with you in your great loss,” he said, without looking round.

00:15:03.159 --> 00:15:08.375
“I beg that you will understand I am only their servant and merely obeying orders.”

00:15:08.375 --> 00:15:21.261
There was no reply; the old woman’s face was white, her eyes staring, and her breath inaudible; on the husband’s face was a look such as his friend the sergeant might have carried into his first action.

00:15:21.261 --> 00:15:27.305
“I was to say that ‘Maw and Meggins’ disclaim all responsibility,” continued the other.

00:15:27.305 --> 00:15:35.943
“They admit no liability at all, but in consideration of your son’s services, they wish to present you with a certain sum as compensation.”

00:15:35.943 --> 00:15:36.368
Mr.

00:15:36.368 --> 00:15:42.694
White dropped his wife’s hand, and rising to his feet, gazed with a look of horror at his visitor.

00:15:42.694 --> 00:15:45.712
His dry lips shaped the words, “How much?”

00:15:45.712 --> 00:15:48.235
“Two hundred pounds,” was the answer.

00:15:48.235 --> 00:15:58.446
Unconscious of his wife’s shriek, the old man smiled faintly, put out his hands like a sightless man, and dropped, a senseless heap, to the floor.

00:15:58.446 --> 00:16:08.151
In the huge new cemetery, some two miles distant, the old people buried their dead, and came back to a house steeped in shadow and silence.

00:16:08.151 --> 00:16:22.040
It was all over so quickly that at first they could hardly realize it, and remained in a state of expectation as though of something else to happen —something else which was to lighten this load, too heavy for old hearts to bear.

00:16:22.040 --> 00:16:31.562
But the days passed, and expectation gave place to resignation—the hopeless resignation of the old, sometimes miscalled, apathy.

00:16:31.562 --> 00:16:38.881
Sometimes they hardly exchanged a word, for now they had nothing to talk about, and their days were long to weariness.

00:16:39.496 --> 00:16:47.393
It was about a week after that the old man, waking suddenly in the night, stretched out his hand and found himself alone.

00:16:47.393 --> 00:16:52.627
The room was in darkness, and the sound of subdued weeping came from the window.

00:16:52.627 --> 00:16:55.224
He raised himself in bed and listened.

00:16:55.224 --> 00:16:57.499
“Come back,” he said, tenderly.

00:16:57.499 --> 00:16:58.727
“You will be cold.”

00:16:58.727 --> 00:17:03.523
“It is colder for my son,” said the old woman, and wept afresh.

00:17:03.523 --> 00:17:06.633
The sound of her sobs died away on his ears.

00:17:06.633 --> 00:17:09.868
The bed was warm, and his eyes heavy with sleep.

00:17:09.868 --> 00:17:16.448
He dozed fitfully, and then slept until a sudden wild cry from his wife awoke him with a start.

00:17:16.448 --> 00:17:18.965
“The paw!” she cried wildly.

00:17:18.965 --> 00:17:20.292
“The monkey’s paw!”

00:17:20.292 --> 00:17:22.070
He started up in alarm.

00:17:22.070 --> 00:17:22.727
“Where?

00:17:22.727 --> 00:17:23.543
Where is it?

00:17:23.543 --> 00:17:24.579
What’s the matter?”

00:17:24.579 --> 00:17:27.466
She came stumbling across the room toward him.

00:17:27.466 --> 00:17:29.939
“I want it,” she said, quietly.

00:17:29.939 --> 00:17:31.307
“You’ve not destroyed it?”

00:17:31.307 --> 00:17:35.531
“It’s in the parlour, on the bracket,” he replied, marvelling.

00:17:35.531 --> 00:17:36.191
“Why?”

00:17:36.191 --> 00:17:40.772
She cried and laughed together, and bending over, kissed his cheek.

00:17:40.772 --> 00:17:44.175
“I only just thought of it,” she said, hysterically.

00:17:44.175 --> 00:17:46.142
“Why didn’t I think of it before?

00:17:46.142 --> 00:17:47.594
Why didn’t you think of it?”

00:17:47.594 --> 00:17:49.594
“Think of what?” he questioned.

00:17:49.594 --> 00:17:52.801
“The other two wishes,” she replied, rapidly.

00:17:52.801 --> 00:17:54.210
“We’ve only had one.”

00:17:54.210 --> 00:17:57.367
“Was not that enough?” he demanded, fiercely.

00:17:57.367 --> 00:18:01.210
“No,” she cried, triumphantly; “we’ll have one more.

00:18:01.210 --> 00:18:05.274
Go down and get it quickly, and wish our boy alive again.”

00:18:05.274 --> 00:18:10.182
The man sat up in bed and flung the bedclothes from his quaking limbs.

00:18:10.182 --> 00:18:13.951
“Good God, you are mad!” he cried, aghast.

00:18:13.951 --> 00:18:20.058
“Get it,” she panted; “get it quickly, and wish—Oh, my boy, my boy!”

00:18:20.058 --> 00:18:23.038
Her husband struck a match and lit the candle.

00:18:23.038 --> 00:18:26.105
“Get back to bed,” he said, unsteadily.

00:18:26.105 --> 00:18:27.910
“You don’t know what you are saying.”

00:18:27.910 --> 00:18:33.805
“We had the first wish granted,” said the old woman, feverishly; “why not the second?”

00:18:33.805 --> 00:18:36.616
“A coincidence,” stammered the old man.

00:18:36.616 --> 00:18:41.239
“Go and get it and wish,” cried his wife, quivering with excitement.

00:18:41.239 --> 00:18:45.381
The old man turned and regarded her, and his voice shook.

00:18:45.381 --> 00:18:53.253
“He has been dead ten days, and besides he—I would not tell you else, but—I could only recognize him by his clothing.

00:18:53.253 --> 00:18:57.046
If he was too terrible for you to see then, how now?”

00:18:57.046 --> 00:19:01.664
“Bring him back,” cried the old woman, and dragged him toward the door.

00:19:01.664 --> 00:19:04.163
“Do you think I fear the child I have nursed?”

00:19:04.163 --> 00:19:09.713
He went down in the darkness, and felt his way to the parlour, and then to the mantelpiece.

00:19:09.713 --> 00:19:24.439
The talisman was in its place, and a horrible fear that the unspoken wish might bring his mutilated son before him ere he could escape from the room seized upon him, and he caught his breath as he found that he had lost the direction of the door.

00:19:24.439 --> 00:19:34.766
His brow cold with sweat, he felt his way round the table, and groped along the wall until he found himself in the small passage with the unwholesome thing in his hand.

00:19:34.766 --> 00:19:38.262
Even his wife’s face seemed changed as he entered the room.

00:19:38.262 --> 00:19:43.764
It was white and expectant, and to his fears seemed to have an unnatural look upon it.

00:19:43.764 --> 00:19:45.324
He was afraid of her.

00:19:45.324 --> 00:19:48.191
“Wish!” she cried, in a strong voice.

00:19:48.191 --> 00:19:50.929
“It is foolish and wicked,” he faltered.

00:19:51.529 --> 00:19:53.551
“Wish!” repeated his wife.

00:19:53.551 --> 00:19:54.906
He raised his hand.

00:19:54.906 --> 00:19:57.055
“I wish my son alive again.”

00:19:57.055 --> 00:20:01.591
The talisman fell to the floor, and he regarded it fearfully.

00:20:01.591 --> 00:20:08.933
Then he sank trembling into a chair as the old woman, with burning eyes, walked to the window and raised the blind.

00:20:08.933 --> 00:20:16.559
He sat until he was chilled with the cold, glancing occasionally at the figure of the old woman peering through the window.

00:20:16.559 --> 00:20:28.786
The candle-end, which had burned below the rim of the china candlestick, was throwing pulsating shadows on the ceiling and walls, until, with a flicker larger than the rest, it expired.

00:20:28.786 --> 00:20:41.127
The old man, with an unspeakable sense of relief at the failure of the talisman, crept back to his bed, and a minute or two afterward the old woman came silently and apathetically beside him.

00:20:41.127 --> 00:20:45.734
Neither spoke, but lay silently listening to the ticking of the clock.

00:20:45.734 --> 00:20:50.764
A stair creaked, and a squeaky mouse scurried noisily through the wall.

00:20:50.764 --> 00:21:00.812
The darkness was oppressive, and after lying for some time screwing up his courage, he took the box of matches, and striking one, went downstairs for a candle.

00:21:00.812 --> 00:21:12.822
At the foot of the stairs the match went out, and he paused to strike another; and at the same moment a knock, so quiet and stealthy as to be scarcely audible, sounded on the front door.

00:21:12.822 --> 00:21:16.354
The matches fell from his hand and spilled in the passage.

00:21:16.354 --> 00:21:21.113
He stood motionless, his breath suspended until the knock was repeated.

00:21:21.113 --> 00:21:26.638
Then he turned and fled swiftly back to his room, and closed the door behind him.

00:21:26.638 --> 00:21:29.198
A third knock sounded through the house.

00:21:29.198 --> 00:21:32.324
“What’s that?” cried the old woman, starting up.

00:21:32.324 --> 00:21:36.521
“A rat,” said the old man in shaking tones—“a rat.

00:21:36.521 --> 00:21:38.507
It passed me on the stairs.”

00:21:38.507 --> 00:21:40.788
His wife sat up in bed listening.

00:21:40.788 --> 00:21:43.656
A loud knock resounded through the house.

00:21:43.656 --> 00:21:45.671
“It’s Herbert!” she screamed.

00:21:45.671 --> 00:21:46.672
“It’s Herbert!”

00:21:46.672 --> 00:21:52.995
She ran to the door, but her husband was before her, and catching her by the arm, held her tightly.

00:21:52.995 --> 00:21:56.159
“What are you going to do?” he whispered hoarsely.

00:21:56.159 --> 00:22:00.768
“It’s my boy; it’s Herbert!” she cried, struggling mechanically.

00:22:00.768 --> 00:22:03.076
“I forgot it was two miles away.

00:22:03.076 --> 00:22:04.647
What are you holding me for?

00:22:04.647 --> 00:22:05.505
Let go.

00:22:05.505 --> 00:22:07.181
I must open the door.”

00:22:07.181 --> 00:22:11.743
“For God’s sake don’t let it in,” cried the old man, trembling.

00:22:11.743 --> 00:22:15.162
“You’re afraid of your own son,” she cried, struggling.

00:22:15.162 --> 00:22:16.147
“Let me go.

00:22:16.147 --> 00:22:18.577
I’m coming, Herbert; I’m coming.”

00:22:18.577 --> 00:22:20.929
There was another knock, and another.

00:22:20.929 --> 00:22:25.439
The old woman with a sudden wrench broke free and ran from the room.

00:22:25.439 --> 00:22:31.428
Her husband followed to the landing, and called after her appealingly as she hurried downstairs.

00:22:31.428 --> 00:22:37.343
He heard the chain rattle back and the bottom bolt drawn slowly and stiffly from the socket.

00:22:37.343 --> 00:22:40.646
Then the old woman’s voice, strained and panting.

00:22:40.646 --> 00:22:43.315
“The bolt,” she cried, loudly.

00:22:43.315 --> 00:22:44.220
“Come down.

00:22:44.220 --> 00:22:45.389
I can’t reach it.”

00:22:45.389 --> 00:22:51.208
But her husband was on his hands and knees groping wildly on the floor in search of the paw.

00:22:51.208 --> 00:22:54.999
If he could only find it before the thing outside got in.

00:22:54.999 --> 00:23:04.211
A perfect fusillade of knocks reverberated through the house, and he heard the scraping of a chair as his wife put it down in the passage against the door.

00:23:04.211 --> 00:23:13.805
He heard the creaking of the bolt as it came slowly back, and at the same moment he found the monkey’s paw, and frantically breathed his third and last wish.

00:23:14.420 --> 00:23:19.445
The knocking ceased suddenly, although the echoes of it were still in the house.

00:23:19.445 --> 00:23:22.454
He heard the chair drawn back, and the door opened.

00:23:22.454 --> 00:23:33.342
A cold wind rushed up the staircase, and a long loud wail of disappointment and misery from his wife gave him courage to run down to her side, and then to the gate beyond.

00:23:33.342 --> 00:23:38.389
The street lamp flickering opposite shone on a quiet and deserted road.
